title: Calls for a ceasefire!
new-page: true
view-if: year = 1917 and month = 11 and week = 2
max-visits: 1
face-image: 
tags: event

= Calls for a Ceasefire

The leaders of Vikzhel have called a conference of all "democratic" groups in the capital, for the intentions of negotiating a mutually acceptable and inclusive socialist coalition government. This comes off the heels of Vikzhel's protest at the 2nd Soviet Congress, and after a week of pleas in the press for an immediate "ceasefire" between the Bolshevik and anti-Bolshevik forces, issued by the Left-SRs, Menshevik Internationalists, various unions, and other self-described "neutral" factions. Unless all parties cease their conflict and begin serious negotiations, Vikzhel has pledged to call a nationwide railway strike, which has the potential to cripple our transportation capabilities. 

Surprisingly, Vikzhel's demands are echoed by the Petrograd Peasants' Soviet, Petrograd Trade Union Council, Central Soviet of Factory-Shop Committees, and countless unions, soviets, and committees, despite these the Bolshevik majorities within them. Although Lenin dismisses the talks as mere "diplomatic smokescreen for military operations", the Central Committee seems convinced that there is no choice but to open negotiations. 

Yet at the same time, the precariousness of the Bolshevik position has only hardened the intransigence of many moderate socialists, with some right Mensheviks and SRs going so far as to demand Lenin and Trotsky be barred from any future cabinet, or for the Bolsheviks to be excluded from the all-socialist government entirely. For as much as the Leninists refuse to work with the other parties, the other parties have grown just as obtuse, making the search for an amicable solution all the more difficult. 


- @refuse_vikzhel
- @accept_vikzhel

@refuse_vikzhel
title: We refuse to be blackmailed by counterrevolutionaries! 
subtitle: Reject the offer to attend the conference. 
on-arrival:

Our refusal to cooperate entirely shocks the other parties, seemingly proving the right socialists correct after all—more than likely compromise will be impossible from here on out. Likewise, we receive immense condemnation from various interest groups, and even some members of our own party.

Though on the other hand, Lenin's argument against cooperation carries some sway in the working-class districts of the capital, with workers' councils agreeing that they cannot compromise the values and gains of the October Revolution. 

Vikzhel immediately unleashes a full-scale strike across the railway network, and many other prominent unions and enterprises decide to join them. 



@accept_vikzhel
title: There is no harm in hearing them out...
subtitle: Send representatives to the conference. 
on-arrival: influence = (10 + (left_m_relation / 10 ) - (instability / 20));
go-to: menu

Though talks initially stall, several days of arguing (and news of anti-Bolshevik military failures) wear down the most ardent anti-Bolshevik representatives. Though the promises we make here won't be binding until signed-off on by the Party, we are in a position to potentially reach a compromise. 


